Huffman tree chosen on the fly (levels 3 and above)
===================================================

Follows experiments/statskip, sections 13 and 14, where the choice was
simulated (treesim) then prototyped (SLZ_AUTO_TREE). The work went through
several versions of the series, all restarting from master (v1.4-dev5):

- the first series (sections 1 to 20):
    OPTIM: slz: derive the literal skip ramp of levels 1 and 2 from plit
    OPTIM: slz: derive level 3's literal skip ramp from plit too
    slz: renumber the compression levels to make room for a new level 3
    MEDIUM: slz: choose the huffman tree on the fly from level 3
  then more commits named in the sections below. Level 3 is a copy of level
  2 with the automatic choice, the former levels 3 and 4 become 4 and 5 with
  it too, and levels 1 and 2 are unchanged, since speed matters less from
  level 3 on;
- the second series, a clean version of the first, and the third series,
  with one encoder function (section 21);
- the final series (sections 21 to 26), which is the one merged: 4 levels,
  the tree chosen from level 2, one encoder function without slz-enc.h,
  streams starting with the text tree, and the content type kept as a hint.

Commits of the earlier series are named by their subjects followed by the
series they belong to. Two experiments are kept as patches: ramp-l1.patch
(section 22) and auto-tbl.patch (section 27).

All measurements: Xeon W-2145, CPU 2, gcc 13 -O3 -march=native with
-Wa,-mbranches-within-32B-boundaries -Wa,-malign-branch=jcc+fused+jmp+call,
best of 5 (experiments/statskip/bench.sh) unless stated otherwise.


1. Keeping levels 1 and 2 identical
-----------------------------------

The first version passed <autom>, <start> and the shared table to the single
always-inline encoder, constant for levels 1 and 2. Their output was the
same, but their code was not: measured with epad.sh (4 .text paddings x 3
runs, builds alternated), level 1 lost 0.2-0.3% and level 2 0.6-0.8%,
consistently over all paddings. Bisecting showed no single culprit: each
piece of auto-only code, dead once the constants are folded, changed the
register allocation, and only removing all of them restored it. gcc runs
its early passes on the body before inlining it, while <autom> is still a
variable.

The encoder's body thus moved to src/slz-enc.h, included twice by slz.c,
with SLZ_ENC_AUTO = 0 (_slz_rfc1951_encode, levels 1-2 and -T) and 1
(_slz_rfc1951_encode_auto), the auto-only parts under #if. Levels 1 and 2
then produce exactly the same instructions as before (padding prefixes
aside), and epad.sh measures -0.08% to +0.23% on silesia, web and linux.

The text tree's encoder for levels 3-5 is a single noinline function shared
by the three levels: with one inlined copy per level, level 5 was 6% slower
than level 4 on linux and web (code placement), and sharing it costs
nothing at levels 3-4 (silesia -5: 449 -> 463, linux 498 -> 527, web
704 -> 743 MB/s).


2. Results
----------

Output and speed vs the level each one replaces ("choose the huffman tree
on the fly from level 3" vs "renumber the compression levels", first
series):

               silesia          doc.tar          linux 200MB      web
  -3 (was -2)  -6.02% 541->497  -2.37% 1040->950  -12.55% 567->526 -11.80% 800->742
  -4 (was -3)  -5.99% 514->478  -2.37%  899->834  -12.30% 559->528 -11.62% 788->744
  -5 (was -4)  -5.98% 496->463  -2.39%  653->588  -12.23% 557->527 -11.56% 788->743

Level 3 is 1.7% smaller than -T on silesia and 4.6% faster, the same size
on text and 2.6% slower. Levels 1, 2 and -T are unchanged (same output,
same code). Round trips checked with gzip, zlib and raw deflate at levels
3-5, and with 1 kB blocks (switches across calls).


3. Cheaper fixed tree accounting (fixed-bit7.patch, rejected)
------------------------------------------------------------

Replacing the per-literal comparison of both trees' code lengths, in the
fixed tree's out-of-line literal emitter, by bit 7 alone (+1.5 bits per
byte < 0x80, -2 bits above, switch at 1280 bits): silesia -3 +0.18% larger
and 0.7% faster (499 -> 503), doc.tar 0.09% smaller, same speed. The
accounting of the fixed tree is not where the time goes.


4. Counting <lit_excess> at the match (text-lazy.patch, rejected)
------------------------------------------------------------------

Avoiding the text tree's per-literal <lit_excess> in the fast path was
tried. In the text encoder of levels 3-5, the literals are counted
instead at each match, from a position <lacc> up to which they are already
counted, and the binary test every 64 literals counts the bytes >= 0x80
among the last 64 (SWAR, switch at 16 or more). Same output within 0.002%,
but slower everywhere: silesia -3 499 -> 486, doc.tar 954 -> 922, linux
528 -> 507, web 744 -> 718 MB/s (-2.5 to -4%). The add per literal in the
loop costs less than a separate loop at each match.


5. Where the remaining cost is
------------------------------

On linux, nearly all of it in the text tree: master -T (level 4's matcher,
non-shared table) runs at 540 MB/s, level 3 at 526-528, level 2 at 567.
So 5% is the text tree itself and 2.4% the shared table (its address is
an argument of the text encoder, held in a register, where -T addresses
its own table from the stack pointer).


6. Why silesia and doc.tar lose more speed than size
----------------------------------------------------

Except on linux and web, level 3 lost more speed than size. Measured on
the Xeon W, at -3, MB/s, best of 5 alternated runs. The "linux" file here
is linux-7.0.tar (719 MB/s at the old level 3, not the lin200 sample of
section 2). Builds:
  r   = "renumber the compression levels" (first series, no automatic
        tree)
  c   = "choose the huffman tree on the fly from level 3" (first series)
  ff  = c with SLZ_AUTO_IN = 1<<30: never leaves the fixed tree, same
        output as r, but pays all the automatic tree's plumbing
  na  = ff without the fixed tree's accounting
  tt  = c starting with the text tree and never leaving it

              r       na      ff      c       tt
  silesia   541.1   528.3   507.3   499.5   460.6
  doc.tar  1036.4  1014.5   982.4   953.2   593.7
  linux     719.4   706.2   687.5   671.7   672.7
  web       800.1   785.1   765.9   744.9   750.5

So at -3 on silesia, the 7.7% loss splits into about 2.4% for the
structure (r -> na), 4% for the fixed tree's accounting (na -> ff,
including 0.8% for the call to auto_account() alone), and only 1.5% for
the text tree itself (ff -> c). These costs are paid on every byte the
fixed tree encodes, whether or not the text tree is ever chosen, so they
weigh most where the gain is smallest. doc.tar's text segments really are
text (674 of 690 segments have fewer than 1% bytes >= 0x80, mostly 4 kB or
larger), so there's no ping-pong between trees. The text tree's encoder
taking 63% of the time for 38% of the bytes is normal: the fixed tree
skips the PNG images, while the text bytes go through the matcher.

The text encoder's level doesn't matter: level 2, 3 or 5 matching for
the text tree is within +0.4% / -1.2% in speed and 0.1% in size of
level 4.

Two fixes, both with unchanged output and same code for levels 1-2:

- "let the fixed tree's encoder own the reference table from level 3"
  (first series): the wrapper owning the reference table passed it by
  pointer to the fixed tree's encoder, and once inlined gcc kept that
  pointer in %r14 instead of addressing the table from %rsp, spilling the
  stream pointer (54 stack accesses in level 3 vs 35 in level 2). An empty
  asm on the pointer, or inlining the text encoder in level 3 as well,
  didn't change that. Now the fixed tree's encoder owns the table and
  calls the text one. With no accounting and no switch, level 3 is then
  within 0.5% of r (538.5 vs 541.1 on silesia). This replaces the 2.4%
  "shared table" of section 5.

- "account the text tree's savings with one addition per literal" (first
  series): the fixed tree's accounting took two lookups, a subtraction and
  an addition per literal. A 64-bit fixed_auto[] table holding the code in
  its low half and the saved bits + 16 in its high half leaves a single
  64-bit addition. Forced to the fixed tree: no accounting 539.3, old
  accounting 521.6, 32-bit packed table (16+16, shift and mask) 514.7 on
  the base before the previous fix, 64-bit table 528.3. Section 3's
  conclusion was wrong: the fixed tree's accounting did cost about 4%, and
  bit 7 alone was still an operation per literal.

Result, MB/s (output identical to c):

          silesia               doc.tar               linux                web
        r     c    now        r     c    now        r     c    now        r     c    now
  -3  541.2 498.3 510.1   1035.3 952.6 962.7    719.0 671.8 674.7    798.9 744.0 746.8
  -4  513.4 478.0 489.9    894.9 832.7 840.6    705.7 669.1 672.9    787.7 744.4 747.3
  -5  495.9 462.7 476.2    653.4 604.7 630.8    706.6 671.0 674.2    786.9 744.5 745.8

So -3 now loses 5.8% speed for 6.0% size on silesia, but still 7.0% for
2.4% on doc.tar. What's left: about 2% for the accounting even with one
addition per literal, and the text tree's own cost (rs64ff -> now: 3.7%
silesia, 5.2% doc.tar, 4.9% linux, 5.2% web).

Rejected:

- probe.patch (against c): no accounting in the fixed tree at all.
  It runs for a budget of bytes, then tries the text tree, whose own
  accounting (already free) decides to stay or not, doubling the budget
  after each failed attempt (text run shorter than SLZ_PROBE_OK). doc.tar
  wants frequent attempts and silesia rare ones, and no setting matches
  the accounting on both: 16k-1M-16k gives silesia +0.57% larger, doc.tar
  +1.37%; 1k-64k-1k gives doc.tar +0.00% but silesia +1.80%. It's only
  faster where it's larger, since it spends more time in the fixed tree.

- Sampling one literal in 4 for the accounting: same speed as the 32-bit
  packed table, +0.01% on silesia, -0.1 to -0.3% on doc.tar and linux.

About removing level T (and the ctype argument): the encoder's body still
needs ctype as a constant for the text instance of levels 3-5, but once
the forced text tree is gone it could be derived from SLZ_ENC_AUTO (2 for
text, 0-1 for fixed). That wouldn't change the code generated, since it's
already constant in each instance. Not done.


7. All levels, before and after
-------------------------------

The ratios and speeds of all 5 levels on the 4 corpora. "Before" is r
(levels renumbered, -3 identical to -2), "now" is the one-addition
accounting above. Xeon W, MB/s, best of 5 alternated runs, ratio = output /
input. -T is from r, for reference.

                 before          now          now vs before
  silesia     ratio   MB/s   ratio   MB/s    size     speed
    -1       46.61%  621.5  46.61%  622.4   +0.00%   +0.14%
    -2       43.56%  540.8  43.56%  539.5   +0.00%   -0.24%
    -3       43.56%  541.1  40.93%  509.8   -6.02%   -5.78%
    -4       43.18%  513.3  40.59%  489.6   -5.99%   -4.62%
    -5       43.11%  496.4  40.53%  476.4   -5.98%   -4.03%
    -T       41.64%  475.4

  doc.tar
    -1       69.18% 1152.3  69.18% 1154.7   +0.00%   +0.21%
    -2       64.23% 1035.7  64.23% 1035.1   +0.00%   -0.06%
    -3       64.23% 1034.5  62.71%  965.5   -2.37%   -6.67%
    -4       64.51%  897.0  62.98%  849.1   -2.37%   -5.34%
