    -3       20.10%  799.2  17.73%  745.9  -11.80%   -6.67%
    -4       20.05%  787.5  17.72%  744.3  -11.62%   -5.49%
    -5       20.04%  787.1  17.72%  747.2  -11.56%   -5.07%
    -T       17.72%  762.2

Reading it:
- vs -2, the new -3 costs 6-7% speed for 2.4% (doc.tar) to 11.8% (web)
  less output. That's the real price, and -2 stays available as is.
- The new -3 is smaller and faster than the old -4 and -5 on all 4
  corpora (e.g. silesia 40.93% at 510 vs 43.11% at 496, doc.tar 62.71% at
  966 vs 64.01% at 654), so the old -4/-5 have no remaining use.
- vs -T, the new -3 is smaller and faster on silesia (40.93% vs 41.64%,
  510 vs 475) and doc.tar (same ratio within 0.2%, 966 vs 625), and
  slightly larger and slower on linux (20.79% vs 20.61%, -1.4%) and web
  (same ratio, -2.1%).
- The new -4 and -5 bring almost nothing over the new -3: 0.3-0.6 points
  on silesia, under 0.1 point on linux and web, for 4-7% of speed. That is
  a matcher issue, independent of the tree choice.


8. Literal runs with the text tree
----------------------------------

Does plit rarely reach 64 with text? Counted at -3 from
the lengths passed to the text tree's literal emitters (runs replayed
through the recorded references, text_replay(), are not counted):

              runs <8   <16    <32    <64    >=64   lits in runs >=64  64-checks  binary exits
  silesia     95.34%   4.16%  0.47%  0.04%  0.005%   0.16%               211         18
  doc.tar     87.74%   9.80%  2.18%  0.24%  0.03%    0.79%               962        673
  linux       90.89%   7.78%  1.21%  0.10%  0.01%    0.22%              2608          2
  web         91.09%   7.25%  1.38%  0.25%  0.02%    0.81%              1008          2

So it's true: in text, a run of 64 literals is rare (at most 0.03% of the
runs, under 1% of the literals), and the check every 64 literals runs a
few hundred to a few thousand times per corpus. On doc.tar 70% of these
checks leave for the fixed tree, on the other corpora nearly none.


9. Four remarks on sections 7-8
-------------------------------

doc.tar: not a standard corpus. It's a plain tar of /usr/share/doc on
this machine, built for the skipping study (experiments/statskip) because
47% of it is .gz files mixed with short text files, i.e. many short
incompressible areas. It is not representative of what one would try to
compress, which is why it behaves so differently from linux.

linux vs -T (20.79% vs 20.61%): 359 MB of linux-7.0.tar (22%), in 225
zenc calls of 4 MB, stay in the fixed tree, e.g. the AMD register headers
under drivers/gpu/drm/amd/include/asic_reg/. On one such 4 MB chunk, -3
gives 443673 bytes and -T 410846 (-7.4%). There the text tree saves
nothing on literals (upper case and '_' take 8 bits in both trees), the
whole gain is on the length and distance codes, and the fixed tree only
accounted literals. So knowledge was indeed lost. match-acct.patch adds
to <tree_save>, at each match of the fixed tree at levels 3-5, the
difference between both trees' length and distance codes:

            -3 now          + match accounting      -T
  silesia   40.93%  510.1   40.84%  499.4 (-2.1%)   41.64%
  doc.tar   62.71%  965.2   62.56%  954.9 (-1.1%)   62.51%
  linux     20.79%  675.4   20.63%  671.5 (-0.6%)   20.61%
  web       17.73%  749.0   17.72%  749.4 ( 0.0%)   17.72%

It is a load, two lookups and a store per match in the hot loop, written
for the measurement only, not optimized.

Leaving the text tree on plit alone: replacing the 64-literal test
"lit_excess >= plit" by an unconditional exit (like level 1 relying on
plit only) is larger everywhere (silesia +0.03%, doc.tar +0.12%, linux
+0.21%, web +0.38%) and slower (silesia -3.2%, doc.tar -4.5%), from the
extra exits and rescans. And <lit_excess> stays needed per literal anyway
for the stored block decision of the text tree.

Level 1 + automatic tree (level 3 calling the auto encoder with level 1
for the fixed tree; the text tree's encoder stays at level 4, and running
it at level 1 changes nothing in speed and costs 0.2% on linux):

                    l1+auto         l1+auto+match acct
  silesia         43.98%  581.6     43.92%  569.8
  doc.tar         67.73% 1064.6     67.61% 1058.1
  linux           20.80%  676.6     20.64%  672.0
  web             17.73%  748.9     17.73%  747.7

vs level 1 (46.61% 622, 69.18% 1154, 23.36% 726, 20.13% 803), and level
2 (43.56% 540, 64.23% 1035, 23.35% 718, 20.10% 800).

Why levels 4 and 5 are useless, and why l1+auto gives the same speed and
ratio as -3 on linux and web: all of them share the same text tree
encoder (level 4), which encodes nearly all of these files. The level
only applies to the fixed tree's segments. The text encoder itself runs at
670-750 MB/s on these whatever its level (section 6: levels 2, 3 and 5
within about 1%), so that's the ceiling of every automatic level on text.


10. Is it the tree's cost that keeps linux in the fixed tree?
-------------------------------------------------------------

The question: maybe we rarely save enough bits on literals to pay for
the text tree's header, because we look up references too often, and
stopping the lookups after some plit on compressible text would build a
long literal run that pays for it earlier.

Counted with the fixed tree forced (and both accountings measured, not
used), what the text tree would have saved:

                  literals     matches      bytes in    saved on      saved on
                               (count)      matches     literals      matches
  amd 4 MB chunk     35506       155413      4153710     2898 bits    243380 bits
  linux-7.0.tar   88722229    115147824   1553046376    75.4 Mbit    277.1 Mbit
  silesia         27761139     18372598    167529349   -18.7 Mbit     52.6 Mbit

On the AMD headers, the literals are 0.85% of the bytes and the text tree
saves 0.08 bit per literal: there is nothing to gain on them, however long
the runs. 99% of the bytes are in matches, and that's where the text tree
saves its 30 kB (7.4%): its length and distance codes are shorter for this
data. The 624-bit header would be paid back within about 10 kB of it.
Turning matches into literals would cost far more than the header. So the
header isn't what keeps us in the fixed tree. The fixed tree's accounting
just didn't see the matches. Over all of linux, 79% of the text tree's
savings are on matches. On silesia the literals are a net loss, and the
gain is entirely on matches.

Both accountings are needed though: matches only (no literal accounting)
gives linux 338972783 (vs 338950285 with both), web 33252627 (vs 33251594),
but silesia 88221525 (+1.9% vs both) and doc.tar 45238927 (+0.3%).

About the cost of fixed_auto[] (2 kB): the previous code read fixed_huff[]
and text_huff[] (576 bytes each) for each literal. The 1 kB 16+16-bit
version measured slower than the 2 kB one (514.7 vs 528.3 on silesia,
fixed tree forced), from the extra shift and mask. The saved bits per
literal range from -3 to +3, so they would fit in the 3 unused bits of
fixed_huff[] (512 bytes), but extracting them and masking the code costs
the same extra operations as the 16+16 version. Not measured.


11. Text first, the AMD chunk, and the table lookups
----------------------------------------------------

Another idea: considering the text tree's header as a mandatory cost at
the beginning of each block, sending literals with it or as plain (stored)
literals when they don't pay, and the fixed tree only when the literal run
is too short to warrant a switch.

Text tree + stored is what -T does, and treesim's optimum over T and S is
the same as -T within 0.01% (section 13 of experiments/statskip/README).
With level 2's matches, it's 3.29% under -2 on silesia, where the optimum
over F, T and S is 5.66% under -2. So on silesia 2.4 points come from the
fixed tree. It wins in the binary areas with matches (probably the
executables and databases in silesia, not checked per file), where the
literals are short runs between matches
that stored blocks can't take, and where the text tree's codes for bytes
>= 0x80 and for long matches are longer. The fixed tree can't be used for
one short run either: inside a text block it needs the EOB, a fixed block,
then the 624-bit header again to come back. That's why the choice has to
be per area. The optimum counts these transitions exactly and still needs
F. On doc.tar, linux and web, T+S alone is at most 0.04% from the optimum.

The AMD chunk is C headers (register #defines), not firmware:

  slz -2    443673    slz -T   410846
  gzip -1   367046    gzip -6  264706

Its literals are 0.85% of the bytes, so the text tree's literal codes don't
matter there, and its length/distance codes already save 7.4% over the
fixed tree. The remaining 12% to gzip -1 comes from gzip's matching and
its trees computed for this data, not from switching. JavaScript is in
web (js, css, html), where -3 is within 0.03% of -T.

Lookups per literal: in the fixed tree's emitter at levels 3+, only
fixed_auto[] is read (it replaced both fixed_huff[] and text_huff[]). In
the text tree, text_huff[] is read for <lit_excess> then again when
sending, from the same cache line. So it's one table per literal in each
case already.

Per match, the accounting of section 9 read text_len[] (1 kB) and
text_dist_table[] (128 kB). The difference only depends on the length
(259 values, -3 to +7 bits) and on the distance code (32 values, the
extra bits are the same in both trees, -5 to +1), so two int8 tables of
259 and 32 bytes give it (match-acct-small.patch). Same sizes within 706
bytes, and:

            -3 now     big tables       small tables
  silesia   509.7    498.8 (-2.1%)    505.0 (-0.9%)
  doc.tar   964.7    951.9 (-1.3%)    954.9 (-1.0%)
  linux     673.4    669.8 (-0.5%)    670.8 (-0.4%)
  web       747.8    747.2 (-0.1%)    746.3 (-0.2%)

for -0.22%, -0.23%, -0.77% and -0.02% in size.


12. Which level for a level 5
-----------------------------

The proposed layout: 1 and 2 as in master, 3 = 1 + auto tree, 4 = 2 +
auto tree + small-table match accounting, 5 = master's level 3 or 4 with
the same. Measured with match-acct-small.patch, where the first series' -3,
-4 and -5 run master's levels 2, 3 and 4 for the fixed tree (ratio, MB/s):

            master 2 + auto   master 3 + auto   master 4 + auto
  silesia   40.84%  504.4     40.50%  481.8     40.44%  469.7
  doc.tar   62.56%  955.7     62.86%  850.2     62.37%  628.8
  linux     20.63%  670.0     20.63%  670.0     20.63%  670.5
  web       17.72%  747.6     17.72%  747.4     17.72%  747.9

On linux and web the three are the same, since the text tree's encoder
(always at level 4) does nearly everything. On silesia, master's level 3
saves 0.34 point for 4.5% of speed, master's level 4 only 0.06 point more
for another 2.5%. On doc.tar, master's level 3 is larger than level 2 (it
was already the case in master: 64.51% vs 64.23%), and level 4 is 34%
slower than level 2 for 0.19 point.


13. All candidates in one run, and the order of the levels
----------------------------------------------------------

A level 3 made of level 1 + auto tree, faster than level 2 but larger on
silesia, would be confusing since neither level would always be better.
And how does level 4 do without match accounting?
All candidates in one paired run (ratio, MB/s, best of 5, Xeon W); "mc" is
match-acct-small.patch:

              master 1        master 2        1+auto          1+auto+mc       2+auto          2+auto+mc       3+auto+mc
  silesia   46.61%  624.0   43.56%  540.1   43.98%  581.5   43.92%  575.4   40.93%  509.1   40.84%  504.3   40.50%  484.7
  doc.tar   69.18% 1155.8   64.23% 1034.3   67.73% 1067.1   67.61% 1060.5   62.71%  965.4   62.56%  958.9   62.86%  847.0
  linux     23.36%  726.4   23.35%  718.2   20.80%  675.3   20.64%  670.4   20.79%  674.2   20.63%  669.6   20.63%  670.2
  web       20.13%  806.2   20.10%  798.4   17.73%  747.1   17.73%  747.0   17.73%  747.0   17.72%  748.0   17.72%  747.7

- Match accounting makes 2+auto smaller on all four (silesia -0.09 point,
  doc.tar -0.15, linux -0.16, web -0.01) for 0.7-0.9% of speed (none on
  web). Without it, level 4 is less good everywhere, as expected.
- 1+auto is not between levels 2 and 4 in any useful way: on silesia and
  doc.tar it's larger than level 2 (43.98% vs 43.56%, 67.73% vs 64.23%)
  and faster, on linux and web it's much smaller than level 2 and slower,
  and there it's identical to 2+auto (same text encoder). So it's never
  better than level 2 in both size and speed, which is the confusing case.
- 2+auto+mc is smaller and slower than level 2 on all four, so it keeps
  the levels ordered. 3+auto+mc is smaller than it on silesia only,
  identical on linux and web, and larger on doc.tar (as master's level 3
  already was vs level 2).


14. What differentiates levels 1 and 2, and intermediate level 3s
-----------------------------------------------------------------

In the first series, levels 1 and 2 only differ by the probing during a
literal skip (slz-enc.h, "level >= 2 && skip >= 64"): both use the same
skip ramp derived from plit (0, 64, 192, 448, ... up to 8128 bytes), the
same backward match (1 byte) and the same debt accounting (whole groups
charged at once). Level 1 skips blindly, level 2 still looks up and
indexes the last word of each skipped 64-byte chunk and stops at the first
one found in the window. So the 3 points between them on silesia are the
probing.

Two knobs for an intermediate level, each with auto tree and
match-acct-small.patch, at -3 (ratio, MB/s, one paired run):
  c512/c1k/c2k: level 1's blind skip with the ramp capped at 512, 1024 or
                2048 bytes instead of 8192;
  st256/st128:  level 2's probing every 256 or 128 bytes instead of 64.

              1+auto+mc      c2k            c1k            c512           st256          st128          2+auto+mc
  silesia   43.92% 575.4   43.08% 561.1   42.38% 532.6   41.96% 520.0   41.21% 516.3   40.97% 509.8   40.84% 506.3
  doc.tar   67.61% 1060.3  64.87% 965.5   63.85% 929.5   63.17% 903.6   63.03% 936.4   62.72% 941.8   62.56% 958.8
  linux     20.64% 670.4   20.63% 671.0   20.63% 670.7   20.63% 670.2   20.63% 670.0   20.63% 670.9   20.63% 670.7
  web       17.73% 748.1   17.73% 746.9   17.73% 748.4   17.73% 747.6   17.73% 747.4   17.72% 746.6   17.72% 746.9

- On linux and web they're all the same: the text tree's encoder does
  nearly everything and never skips. A level 3 can only differ from level
  4 on mixed or binary data.
- On silesia they all sit on one line, about 0.1 point of ratio per 1% of
  speed, from 1+auto to 2+auto. No variant beats the line.
- c1k is smaller than level 2 and slower on all four (silesia 42.38% at 533
  vs 43.56% at 540), so it would keep levels 2 -> 3 ordered, and it's
  between 2 and 4 on silesia. But on doc.tar it's slower than level 4 (930
  vs 959), as are c512 and the sparser probing (st128 942, st256 936): with
  less probing or shorter blind steps, the .gz files are walked over in
  more, shorter skips. Only c2k is as fast as level 4 there (965).


15. Could 1+auto sit between levels 1 and 2?
--------------------------------------------

The concern is 1+auto itself: smaller than level 2 everywhere on text
but slower there, larger on silesia but faster. To stay between levels 1
and 2 it would have to be faster than level 2 on text too. On text,
levels 1 and 2 run at nearly the same speed (linux 726 vs 718, web 806 vs
798) since the skip almost never triggers, so the text tree's encoder
would have to be as fast as the fixed tree's one, about 7% faster than
now.

Where its time goes, on linux-7.0.tar (1+auto+mc, MB/s, sizes are off for
the stripped builds):

                                   linux           web
  level 1                        23.36%  726.3   20.13%  806.8
  level 2                        23.35%  718.1   20.10%  797.9
  1+auto+mc                      20.64%  671.0   17.73%  747.8
  - no lit_excess per literal    20.76%  672.4   17.86%  747.4
  - and no auto_account per run  20.61%  678.6   17.72%  757.6

The table lookup per literal for <lit_excess> costs nothing measurable.
The accounting per literal run costs 1-1.3%. perf stat on linux, whole
zenc run:

                  cycles    instructions   branches   branch-misses  L1D misses
  level 2         10.67 G     19.45 G       2.476 G     154.3 M        327.7 M
  1+auto+mc       11.40 G     22.82 G       2.461 G     157.8 M        340.6 M
  without both    11.25 G     21.91 G       2.436 G     158.0 M        343.7 M

Same branches, mispredicts and cache misses: the text tree's encoder only
executes 17% more instructions (3.4 G more, about 2 per input byte). With
both removed, 2.45 G remain, about 21 instructions per match (115 M
matches): the text tree's per-match decision (cost with <lit_excess>,
text_lit_stored(), <text_debt> kept in the stream, references recorded
while no block is open, <lit_excess> corrected after a backward
extension). That's where a lighter text path for level 3 would have to
cut.


16. A layout without master's level 2
-------------------------------------

1+auto is better than 1+auto+mc (+1.06% speed for +0.14% size on
silesia), hence the proposal of dropping master's level 2 instead. No new
measurement, numbers from section 13 (one paired run) and, for a possible
level 5, section 12 (another run):

  level  content       silesia          doc.tar          linux            web
  1      master 1      46.61%  624.0    69.18% 1155.8    23.36%  726.4    20.13%  806.2
  2      1+auto        43.98%  581.5    67.73% 1067.1    20.80%  675.3    17.73%  747.1
  3      2+auto+mc     40.84%  504.3    62.56%  958.9    20.63%  669.6    17.72%  748.0
  4      3+auto+mc     40.50%  484.7    62.86%  847.0    20.63%  670.2    17.72%  747.7
  (5     4+auto+mc     40.44%  469.7    62.37%  628.8    20.63%  670.5    17.72%  747.9)
  dropped: master 2    43.56%  540.1    64.23% 1034.3    23.35%  718.2    20.10%  798.4

- Each level is smaller and slower than the previous one, except 3 -> 4 on
  doc.tar (larger), and 2 -> 3 -> 4 on linux and web, which are the same
  within noise (the same text tree encoder does nearly everything).
- Master's level 2 is still faster than 1+auto on linux and web (718 vs
  675, 798 vs 747), but there level 1 is faster still for the same size,
  so it brings nothing on text. On silesia and doc.tar it sits between
  1+auto and 2+auto, as a middle point rather than the best at anything.

Truth table as in the current code (level = proposed level):

  level                     1         2         3         4          (5)
  fixed tree's matcher      master 1  master 1  master 2  master 3   master 4
  literal skip (fixed)      ramp      ramp      ramp +    2048 steps none
                            0..8128   0..8128   64 B      + 256 B
                            blind     blind     probes    probes,
                                                          up to 28 kB
  backward match (bytes)    1         1         1         2          8
  debt per literal (>=144)  no        no        no        yes        yes
  automatic tree            no        yes       yes       yes        yes
  match accounting          no        no        yes       yes        yes

  With the automatic tree, the text tree's encoder is the same for all
  levels: master 3's matcher (backward match 2), no skip, stored blocks
  for literal runs that don't pay, references recorded while no block is
  open, and every 64 literals a switch to the fixed tree if they average
  9 bits or more.


17. Levels 1 to 4 implemented
-----------------------------

"new level layout, automatic tree from level 2, 4 levels" (first series)
sets the layout of section 16 without level 5 (it brings little at an
unbounded cost on incompressible data since it never skips), and "account
the text tree's savings on references from level 3" (first series) adds
the small-table match accounting at levels 3 and 4. The
encoders' <level> argument is back to master's numbers (skip, backward
match and debt settings), mapped to the compression levels in a comment
above _slz_rfc1951_encode_lev1(). Each level's output is identical to the
configuration measured before, level 1's code is unchanged.

Final numbers, Xeon W, best of 5, ratio and MB/s:

  level   silesia          doc.tar          linux            web
  1       46.61%  621.8    69.18% 1151.8    23.36%  724.9    20.13%  805.8
  2       43.98%  581.7    67.73% 1067.8    20.80%  675.8    17.73%  748.2
  3       40.84%  506.9    62.56%  957.4    20.63%  671.3    17.72%  748.3
  4       40.50%  485.5    62.86%  848.8    20.63%  671.4    17.72%  748.2

Next: revisit level 4's literal skip to get closer to master's
level 4 at little cost (e.g. probing every 64 bytes), try an 8-byte
backward match at level 4, and a 2-byte one at level 3.

Note: zenc's default level is 2, which is now level 1 + automatic tree.


18. 2-byte backward match at level 3 (l3-back2.patch)
-----------------------------------------------------

Level 3 is the only user of the encoder's level 2, so it's one entry of
SLZ_BACKWARD_MATCH (1 -> 2). Other levels unchanged (same output). Paired
run, best of 5:

            1 byte           2 bytes          size     speed
  silesia   40.84%  505.1    40.81%  504.8   -0.07%   -0.06%
  doc.tar   62.56%  956.2    62.56%  958.0   -0.01%   +0.19%
  linux     20.63%  671.1    20.63%  671.3   -0.00%   +0.03%
  web       17.72%  748.0    17.72%  745.8   +0.00%   -0.29%

Speed within noise, a small gain on silesia only. On linux and web the
text tree's encoder does nearly everything and already uses 2 bytes (it
runs at the encoder's level 3), so only the fixed tree's segments change.


19. Level 4: probing every 64 bytes and 8-byte backward match
-------------------------------------------------------------

l3-back2.patch (section 18) was dropped. Level 4 is the encoder's level 3:
skip in 2048-byte steps up to 28 kB with a probe every 256 bytes, 2-byte
backward match, debt per literal. The text tree's encoder also runs at the
encoder's level 3, so the 8-byte variants keep it at 2 bytes (otherwise
levels 2-3 would change too). "Probe every 64" was tried both ways:
  A  (l4a.patch):  level 3's skip (ramp up to 8128, probe every 64 bytes);
  A2 (l4a2.patch): level 4's 2048-byte steps, probing every 64 bytes
                   instead of 256;
  B  (l4b.patch):  8-byte backward match, current skip;
  C  (l4c.patch):  A + B.
Levels 1-3 and -T unchanged (same output). Level 4, paired run, best of 5:

            current         A               A2              B               C
  silesia   40.50%  487.0   40.81%  496.8   40.48%  481.4   40.49%  483.7   40.80%  494.3
  doc.tar   62.86%  849.5   62.56%  948.3   62.46%  846.2   62.86%  848.1   62.55%  947.2
  linux     20.63%  670.8   20.63%  670.9   20.63%  671.2   20.63%  670.8   20.63%  671.1
  web       17.72%  747.6   17.72%  749.0   17.72%  748.7   17.72%  747.7   17.72%  747.8

  vs current  size/speed:
  silesia                   +0.76% +2.0%    -0.05% -1.2%    -0.03% -0.7%    +0.72% +1.5%
  doc.tar                   -0.48% +11.6%   -0.64% -0.4%    -0.00% -0.2%    -0.49% +11.5%

- A turns level 4 into level 3 with a 2-byte backward match and the debt
  per literal: on silesia it's 40.81% vs level 3's 40.84%. It's the skip
  that makes level 4 smaller, so A removes the difference between the two
  levels (and C as well).
- B, the 8-byte backward match, gains 0.03% on silesia for 0.7% of speed.
- A2 is the only one going in level 4's direction: 0.05% smaller on
  silesia for 1.2% of speed (master 4 + auto + mc was 40.44% at 470,
  section 12), and 0.64% smaller on doc.tar, where level 4 then becomes
  smaller than level 3 (62.46% vs 62.56%) instead of larger.
- Nothing changes on linux and web (text tree's encoder).

A2 was applied in "probe every 64 skipped bytes at level 4" (first series).
A, B and C were dropped.


20. A2 + 8-byte backward match at level 4
-----------------------------------------

A2+B was missing. l4b.patch on top of A2 (text tree's
encoder kept at 2 bytes, levels 1-3 and -T unchanged). Level 4, paired
run, best of 5:

            A2              A2+B            size      speed
  silesia   40.48%  477.7   40.47%  475.5   -0.032%   -0.46%
  doc.tar   62.46%  845.5   62.45%  844.2   -0.015%   -0.15%
  linux     20.63%  668.1   20.63%  668.8   -0.001%   +0.10%
  web       17.72%  745.0   17.72%  744.2   -0.000%   -0.11%

Same as B alone on the 256-byte probing (section 19): 0.03% on silesia,
speed within noise to -0.5%. The probing doesn't change what the backward
extension finds, the two are independent.

Applied in "extend matches up to 8 bytes backwards at level 4" (first
series), the text tree keeping 2 bytes.


21. One encoder function, without slz-enc.h
-------------------------------------------

The second series (clean version of the first) and the third series. In
the first and second series, the encoder's body was in slz-enc.h,
included three times by slz.c (SLZ_ENC_AUTO 0, 1, 2): fixed tree only,
automatic tree's fixed side, and a noinline text side receiving the fixed
side's reference table by pointer. The triple include was rejected
outright: one function, the tree chosen by "if" blocks the way the literal
skip uses <level>, constant arguments describing behaviour and only used
as conditions, never pointers.

  - Passing the reference table by pointer to one shared inlined body had
    already been measured as a dead end months ago (it turns direct stack
    accesses into indirect loads), and was again here: no retry.
  - First attempt (WIP, on the third series): one loop, a constant <tree>
    plus a runtime <text> variable for the tree in use, a label restarting
    the loop at the switch. Output identical to the second series'
    "choose the huffman tree on the fly from level 2", level 1 at the
    same speed (620.4 -> 620.5 MB/s on silesia, 6 instructions shorter),
    but levels 2-4 slower, MB/s, best of 5 alternated, second series ->
    first attempt:

      level   silesia                  linux-7.0
        2     581.0 -> 540.3 (-7.0%)   650.0 -> 605.1 (-6.9%)
        3     510.3 -> 491.7 (-3.6%)   648.9 -> 626.9 (-3.4%)
        4     490.5 -> 480.9 (-2.0%)   647.5 -> 637.4 (-1.6%)

    Level 2 started at -10.5%: the backward match length had become a
    runtime choice (2 bytes for text, 1 for fixed). backward_match(), an
    inline helper called once per tree with a constant length, brought it
    to -7%. The rest is the "if (text)" tests, not mispredictions: 7.4%
    more instructions and 17% more branches at level 2, same mispredicts.
    60% of level 2's time on silesia is in the text tree, whose code had
    been specialized and now tested <text> at every step. Testing the tree
    only every 64 literals gave more branches, not fewer.
  - The approach that worked (WIP, on the third series): one function with
    two parts, a fixed tree part and a text tree part, each with its own
    loop holding only its own tree's code, and gotos between them
    (switch_to_text, switch_to_fixed: close the block, then jump). Both
    index the same local reference table. Output identical to the second
    series, level 1 407 instructions instead of 409. MB/s, best of 5
    alternated, second series -> two-part encoder:

      level   silesia                  linux-7.0
        1     592.6 -> 593.3 (+0.1%)   694.5 -> 696.8 (+0.3%)
        2     554.1 -> 560.6 (+1.2%)   649.1 -> 658.7 (+1.5%)
        3     491.6 -> 492.4 (+0.2%)   648.6 -> 655.8 (+1.1%)
        4     471.8 -> 478.3 (+1.4%)   647.8 -> 655.8 (+1.2%)
       -T     454.6 -> 451.6 (-0.7%)   652.5 -> 649.6 (-0.4%)

    (-T's code only differs by two instructions; within noise.)

The two-part encoder's argument, first named "autom", became <auto_tree>
in the encoder and <track_save> in the copy_lit*() helpers, where it means
adding what the text tree would have saved on the fixed tree's literals to
<tree_save>, or resetting it when they go out as a stored block.


22. Level 1 on Arrow Lake
-------------------------

Runs on ARL, silesia, -D -1 -l 4 (same output everywhere), best of 4:

  commit                MB/s    vs master   level 1 code
  master (v1.4-dev5)    941.2   -           419 instructions
  plit ramp (1)         932.9   -0.9%       409
  new level 2 (2)       920.2   -2.2%       identical to (1)
  third series' tip     929.9   -1.2%       407

  (1) "derive the literal skip ramp of levels 1 and 2 from plit"
  (2) "shift levels 2 and 3 to 3 and 4 to make room for a new level 2",
      final as "insert a new level between levels 1 and 2, shifting 2 and
      3 to 3 and 4"

(1) and (2) compile level 1 to the same code but differ by 1.4%, so at
least that step is code placement. -falign-functions=64 on the tip was
worse (930.7 MB/s). The suspicion was the plit ramp itself (it was neutral
on the Xeon). ramp-l1.patch gives level 1 master's separate skip_ratio
ramp back while levels 2-4 keep the plit one: level 1's output is
identical, its code is 420 instructions (master 419), and levels 2-4
compile to the third series' code.

ARL binaries of master and of the final series' tip (gcc 15.2 -march=
arrowlake-s -O3 -fomit-frame-pointer, read from their DWARF producer, and
rebuilt to the same code) show the cause. Since (1), gcc keeps
plit + 1 in another register for the ramp and the code after the loop, so
each literal without a match costs an extra "mov %edx,%esi" and a "jmp"
back, and the hash hit becomes a taken jump over the function's exit code,
which gcc placed inside the loop. Master's literal block falls into the
loop head instead, and the hit falls through. gcc 13 on the Xeon makes the
same change, where it costs nothing. With ramp-l1.patch on top of the
final series, level 1's loop is master's again
with gcc 15, its output is master's, and levels 2-4 keep the tip's code
(level 4 only differs by alignment padding).

That was not the cause: on ARL, the patched level 1 gave 916.1 MB/s
against 930.1 for the final series' tip (master 941). Counted on the Xeon
with the ARL binaries (perf, silesia, -D -1), the three run the same number
of instructions within 0.2% (master 3.178G, tip 3.182G, ramp-l1 3.184G);
the tip has 8.4% more branches than master, the patched one 1.8%
more, and branch misses are the same within 2%. So the code is
equivalent and the difference is in how the front end takes the layout.
On the Xeon the same sources built with the ARL build's flags give, best
of 5, MB/s:

                        master   tip      ramp-l1
  ARL build's flags      587.6    558.1    562.5
  + JCC flags            610.6    599.9    603.9
  + JCC flags and
    -falign-labels=64    584.8    585.1    589.5

The JCC erratum explains most of the Xeon gap, and -falign-labels=64
removes the rest (at a cost for all three), which points to branch target
placement, as on the Zen 4.

ARL runs with -falign-labels=64 added, silesia, -D -1:

                          without   with -falign-labels=64
  master                   941.2      914.6
  ramp-l1                  916.1      914.3

Once the branch targets are aligned, master and ramp-l1 run at the
same speed: level 1's 1% on ARL is the placement of branch targets, master
having a lucky layout, and not the code. The ramp experiment is dropped,
level 1 keeps the plit ramp. Levels 2-4 lose 3-5% with the flag (padding),
so it's no fix either.

Same ARL runs at the other levels (third series' tip, MB/s): levels 2-4
are better at similar or better ratios than master's, level 3 at the same
speed as master's 3 with 2.3 points less, level 2 0.4 points smaller than
master's 2 and 9% faster:

  silesia   master                 3rd tip
    1       46.61%  940.1          46.61%  928.0
    2       43.56%  830.0          43.98%  905.3
    3       43.17%  794.4          40.84%  793.3
    4       43.11%  746.9          40.47%  757.0


23. 8-byte backward match in the text tree's part
-------------------------------------------------

Does the 8-byte backward match help text (HTML tags)? Scratch build where
the text tree's part uses 8 bytes, against the third series' tip, Xeon:

                    size      MB/s
  web.cat -2       -0.060%   717.6 -> 710.4  -1.0%
  web.cat -3       -0.060%   724.1 -> 709.2  -2.1%
  web.cat -4       -0.060%   720.1 -> 709.1  -1.5%
  linux-7.0 -2     -0.062%   653.8 -> 648.5  -0.8%
  linux-7.0 -3     -0.066%   658.8 -> 643.8  -2.3%
  linux-7.0 -4     -0.066%   653.9 -> 643.9  -1.5%
  silesia -2       -0.018%   557.3 -> 552.7  -0.8%
  silesia -3       -0.020%   490.4 -> 482.8  -1.5%
  silesia -4       -0.021%   463.2 -> 457.7  -1.2%

A check on haproxy's HTML documentation (hap-org.html, 932561 bytes)
found it brings nothing either. The text tree's part keeps 2 bytes.


24. Starting with the text tree
-------------------------------

On large files, the automatic tree came close to the former forced text
tree (-T) but never reached it. ARL runs with the third series' tip, -D:

               linux-7.0 (-p)     silesia (-l 4)     hap-org.html (-l 1000)
    1          23.36%  1039.4     46.61%  929.0      30.21%  868.2
    2          20.80%  1010.0     43.98%  907.3      26.11%  838.1
    3          20.63%  1010.0     40.84%  795.7      26.10%  840.0
    4          20.63%  1019.3     40.47%  763.2      26.09%  844.6
   -T          20.61%  1019.4     41.64%  755.7      26.08%  844.4

Level 4 is always about as small as -T or smaller, and about as fast or
faster; on silesia level 3 is both faster and smaller than -T. What was
missing was the start: every stream started with the fixed tree, which
took 1280 bits of evidence to switch. On small objects that is a constant
loss of about 160 bytes per stream (8% at 4 kB). Single stream made of the
first N bytes, -D, bytes, third series' tip:

                    -1      -2      -4      -T
  hap-org  2 kB     1197    1197    1222    1060
           4 kB     2277    2240    2094    1932
          16 kB     5756    5206    5060    4899
          64 kB    19523   17361   17216   17054
  web.cat  4 kB     1492    1492    1514    1350
          16 kB     5872    5549    5253    5088
          64 kB    17324   15653   15356   15192

Starting with the text tree from level 2 gives exactly -T's output on
these at levels 2 to 4 (hap-org.html 1060, 1932, 4899, 17054; web.cat
1350 at 4 kB). Level 1 must never start with it: it has no text part,
but flush and finish send the end of block code of <cur_tree>, so the
stream would be corrupt. Hence, in the three init functions:

  strm->cur_tree = (strm->level >= 2) ? SLZ_CT_TEXT : SLZ_CT_GENERIC;

Large files are neutral: silesia, linux and web change by at most 0.0014%
in size, and speed by -0.3% to +0.3% at levels 2-4 (Xeon). ARL runs with
the text start (-D):

               hap-org.html       silesia            linux-7.0
    2          26.08%  837.4      43.98%  904.7      20.80%  1009.3
    3          26.08%  833.0      40.84%  794.8      20.63%  1003.0
    4          26.08%  835.1      40.47%  758.0      20.63%  1007.8

Random data is unchanged (the first literals go out as stored blocks
anyway). The start of a binary object costs about 80 bytes, until the text
part gives up after 64 binary literals: start of an ELF binary, -2, 1155
-> 1234 bytes at 4 kB, 9426 -> 9506 at 16 kB, 36310 -> 36389 at 64 kB.
haproxy mostly compresses text types, so this is a win there.


25. The content type as a hint
------------------------------

The third series had removed slz_set_content_type() and zenc -T, since the
encoder no longer needs to be told. It was brought back: haproxy detects
text from the content-type, and at level 1, which has no text tree, that
benefit would be lost. Level 1 + automatic tree already exists, it's level
2, so the hint only needs a dispatch rule in slz_rfc1951_encode(), not a
new encoder.

Which level for a text stream: level 2 can't reach -T on linux (20.80%
vs 20.61%) even when it starts with the text tree, because it has no match
accounting: once the fixed tree takes over on a binary area, it rarely
notices the low-literal text that follows, such as the AMD headers
(section 11). Level 3 gets 20.63% at about level 2's speed on text. Level
4 was considered (section 24: on linux and HTML it's smaller and faster
than -2 and -3), but level 3 is enough ("fast and highly compressed, not
too heavy skips"), and on non-text data a wrong content type then
only costs level 3's speed.

So SLZ_CT_TEXT makes levels 1 and 2 work as level 3 (level 0 stays
uncompressed), and level 1 starts with the text tree as well. zenc's -T
is that hint, no longer exclusive with the level, so that zenc keeps
testing the internal API: -1 -T and -2 -T give the same output as -3.


26. The final series
--------------------

From master (v1.4-dev5), the content type API never removed, each change
in the commit it belongs to:

  slz: derive the literal skip ramp of levels 1 and 2 from plit
  slz: derive level 3's literal skip ramp from plit too
  slz: remove level 4
  slz: insert a new level between levels 1 and 2, shifting 2 and 3 to 3 and 4
  slz: choose the huffman tree on the fly from level 2
  slz: account the text tree's savings on references from level 3
  slz: probe every 64 skipped bytes at level 4
  slz: extend matches up to 8 bytes backwards at level 4
  slz: pass the backward match length to the encoder

"choose the huffman tree on the fly from level 2" brings the two-part
encoder, the text start and the hint (level 1 -> 2 at that point);
"account the text tree's savings on references from level 3" raises the
hint to level 3 with the match accounting. Level 1's output is master's
throughout. The final tree is the same as the third series plus its "start
with the text tree, and bring back the content type as a hint", apart from
a comment reflow. Each commit was checked for output (round trips at
all levels with and without -T, unchanged levels identical), level
functions' code (objdump), and paired timings, given in each message.

Before (after the new level is inserted, same level number) and after (the
last commit), Xeon W, best of 5 alternated, ratio and MB/s:

  silesia     before           after
    1         46.61%  591.7    46.61%  590.3   -0.2%
    2         46.61%  592.5    43.98%  558.0   -5.8%
