Same sizes as the Xeon, and speeds within 0.3% of the third series' tip
plus text start on ARL (sections 22, 24). Level 1 remains 1.1% below master
(941.2), which is what ramp-l1.patch was meant to test (section 22: it's
branch target placement).

27. fixed_auto[] in 32 bits or less (auto-tbl.patch)
----------------------------------------------------

The code of this experiment (SLZ_AUTO_TBL) is auto-tbl.patch, against the
final series before lit_tsave[] was folded into it.

The concern: fixed_auto[] (256 x u64 = 2 kB) could weigh on
the L1 cache, and cost on 32-bit machines (64-bit loads and additions).
Its low 32 bits hold the fixed code (fixed_huff[]'s format, 13 bits at
most) and its high ones the bits the text tree would save, plus 16; one
64-bit addition per literal sums both, the codes never reaching the high
half (65535 x 0x1fff < 2^29). With 32 bits the codes' sum would spill into
the save field, so each variant reads it separately. The saves only range
from -3 to +3. SLZ_AUTO_TBL selects, in _copy_lit_huff():

  0  fixed_auto[], u64, 2 kB (current)
  A  fixed_auto32[], u32, 1 kB: save + 16 in bits 24-31, code below;
     sum += e >> 24, and the code masked
  B  fixed_huff[] (u16, 576 bytes, already used by level 1) for the code,
     and lit_tsave[], int8, 256 bytes, for the save
  C  fixed_auto16[], u16, 512 bytes: save + 3 in bits 13-15, code below;
     sum += e >> 13, and the code masked

Output identical in all four, 64-bit and 32-bit (-m32), levels 2-4, 4 kB
blocks and 3000-byte inputs, on silesia, linux, web, hap-org.html, random
data and an ELF binary; 32-bit round trips checked.

64-bit, Xeon, best of 5, the four alternated in each round, MB/s:

                 0        A               B               C
  silesia -2   558.1   556.2 -0.3%    558.6 +0.1%    557.7 -0.1%
          -3   489.0   484.5 -0.9%    489.1 +0.0%    488.2 -0.2%
          -4   462.6   459.1 -0.8%    461.9 -0.2%    460.0 -0.6%
  linux   -2   656.6   656.6 +0.0%    656.6 +0.0%    656.5 -0.0%
          -3   658.9   658.4 -0.1%    659.0 +0.0%    659.0 +0.0%
          -4   652.2   652.3 +0.0%    649.9 -0.4%    650.4 -0.3%
  web     -2   721.1   720.1 -0.1%    719.4 -0.2%    722.8 +0.2%
          -3   724.3   724.9 +0.1%    726.0 +0.2%    728.1 +0.5%
          -4   720.0   719.7 -0.0%    717.6 -0.3%    717.5 -0.3%
  hap-org -2   547.7   549.7 +0.4%    548.4 +0.1%    548.5 +0.1%
          -3   555.3   553.6 -0.3%    552.5 -0.5%    555.7 +0.1%
          -4   549.5   548.7 -0.1%    547.1 -0.4%    548.6 -0.2%

32-bit (-m32, same machine): the first run showed up to +9% at level 2 on
text for B and C, where the table isn't even read (text tree). Levels 2-4
compile to identical code in the four builds, but B and C move them by 32
bytes, as the literal function changes size. With -falign-functions=64
(all level functions at the same offset, only the literal function
differs), MB/s:

                 0        A               B               C
  silesia -2   408.1   414.1 +1.5%    409.0 +0.2%    391.4 -4.1%
          -3   348.8   352.7 +1.1%    347.4 -0.4%    328.3 -5.9%
          -4   326.4   331.0 +1.4%    330.0 +1.1%    303.8 -6.9%
  linux   -2   482.3   482.3 +0.0%    482.0 -0.1%    480.7 -0.3%
          -3   465.0   464.8 -0.0%    464.2 -0.2%    464.8 -0.0%
          -4   466.1   465.7 -0.1%    466.0 -0.0%    465.6 -0.1%
  hap-org -2   397.2   397.4 +0.1%    397.8 +0.2%    396.8 -0.1%
          -3   385.5   385.5 +0.0%    384.7 -0.2%    383.8 -0.4%
          -4   386.1   386.6 +0.1%    387.2 +0.3%    386.8 +0.2%

C's loss is an i386 register shortage: gcc spills the 16-bit entry
("mov %dx,(%esp)") and reloads it on 32 bits ("mov (%esp),%ecx"), a store
that can't be forwarded to the wider load, on every literal.

- 64-bit: no gain from any variant; the 2 kB table shows no L1 effect on
  the Xeon (32 kB L1D, the benchmark alone). Under haproxy, with other code
  competing for L1, it could differ; that isn't measured here.
- 32-bit x86: A gains 1.1-1.5% where the fixed tree's literals matter
  (silesia), B is neutral, C loses 4-7%. Text is unaffected.
- Not measured here: ARL, and 32-bit ARM, which has more registers than
  i386. The runs below cover ARL and the Cortex-A72 (64-bit).
- If a variant is taken, A halves the table and is the only one faster on
  32-bit; B has the smallest footprint (256 bytes beyond fixed_huff[]) and
  is neutral. mktext would then generate the chosen table instead of
  deriving it from fixed_auto[] as this experiment does.

Runs on other machines, silesia, -D, one run each, MB/s:

  Cortex-A72 (-p)  master   0       A               B
    1              167.1    167.6   168.7 +0.7%     169.7 +1.3%
    2              144.3    160.4   162.1 +1.1%     160.8 +0.2%
    3              139.0    136.8   137.9 +0.8%     137.9 +0.8%
    4              133.1    131.4   132.1 +0.5%     130.9 -0.4%

  ARL (-l 4)                0       A               B
    1                       929.7   928.1 -0.2%     930.5 +0.1%
    2                       905.4   904.8 -0.1%     905.8 +0.0%
    3                       792.9   791.6 -0.2%     794.9 +0.3%
    4                       757.5   754.5 -0.4%     757.2 -0.0%

Level 1 doesn't use the table and its code is the same in all builds, yet
it moves by 1.3% on the A72 and 0.3% on ARL: that's the noise of these
single runs. Within it, A is about 1% faster on the A72 and slightly
slower on ARL (as on the Xeon, -0.8% at levels 3-4 on silesia), B is
neutral on both. B is neutral everywhere measured (Xeon 64 and 32-bit,
ARL, A72), with the smallest footprint and no new code table, only the
256-byte lit_tsave[] next to fixed_huff[]: it's the one to take.


Runs on ARL, EPYC (gcc 15) and Altra, silesia, -D -l 2, best of 4
consecutive runs of each build, MB/s (C = SLZ_AUTO_TBL=3):

  level 1          0        A               B               C
    ARL          931.8    931.4 -0.0%     931.3 -0.1%     932.3 +0.1%
    EPYC         567.0    571.0 +0.7%     571.5 +0.8%     572.6 +1.0%
  level 3
    ARL          794.9    792.3 -0.3%     795.2 +0.0%     787.9 -0.9%
    EPYC         480.5    475.7 -1.0%     475.9 -1.0%     472.4 -1.7%
    Altra        312.5    315.9 +1.1%     314.1 +0.5%     313.2 +0.2%

(Altra didn't differ at level 1.) Level 1 doesn't read the table and its
code is the same in all builds, so EPYC's +0.7 to +1.0% there is layout,
as large as the level 3 differences on EPYC and Altra, which therefore
can't be told from layout. ARL's level 1 is flat (±0.1%), so its level 3
column is the most telling: C -0.9%, A -0.3%, B equal. C is the slowest
at level 3 on both x86 machines, despite its level 1 lead. B is equal on
ARL, slightly ahead on Altra, and 1% behind on EPYC, where A is too.

Run on the Cortex-A720 (gcc 15), silesia, -D -3 -l 2, best of 4,
MB/s:

                 0        A               B               C
  level 3      441.9    448.6 +1.5%     450.2 +1.9%     449.2 +1.7%

There the 64-bit table is clearly the slowest. B is the only variant never
clearly worse anywhere (equal on ARL, the Xeon and 32-bit x86, 1% behind
on EPYC within its layout noise, ahead on the Altra and A720), with the
smallest footprint: it was folded into "choose the huffman tree on the fly
from level 2", mktext -g generating lit_tsave[] instead of fixed_auto[].
The output of every commit of the series is unchanged, and the level
functions compile to the same code (level 4 only differs by placement).

